      "text": "# Community Garden Operating Agreement & Management Plan\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\nThis document establishes a transparent, inclusive, and financially sustainable framework for the community garden. It addresses the current imbalance between demand (40 active + 10 waitlisted households) and supply (30 plots), ensures financial viability through a revised fee structure, and mandates accessibility in both physical access and volunteer roles.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Inclusive Operating Agreement\n\n### Article I: Mission and Principles\n**1.1 Purpose:** To provide accessible, sustainable, and community-driven gardening space for residents of all abilities and backgrounds.\n**1.2 Core Values:** Equity, Accessibility, Transparency, and Sustainability.\n\n### Article II: Plot Allocation and Tenure\n**2.1 Plot Definition:** A \"plot\" is defined as a 4x8 ft raised bed or equivalent ground space.\n**2.2 Tenure:** Plots are leased on an annual basis, renewing each January 1st. Permanent structures (sheds, fences >2ft) are prohibited. Temporary structures (trellises, row covers) are permitted if removed at season\u2019s end.\n**2.3 Allocation Priority:**\n   - **Priority 1:** Households with disabilities or mobility limitations requiring ground-level or raised-bed access.\n   - **Priority 2:** Households with children under 12.\n   - **Priority 3:** General community members.\n   - **Waitlist:** Managed via a transparent lottery system (see Section 4).\n\n### Article III: Accessibility and Inclusion\n**3.1 Physical Accessibility:**\n   - All common paths must be maintained at a minimum width of 36 inches and a slope of <5% to accommodate wheelchairs and walkers.\n   - At least 5 plots must be designated as \"Accessible Plots\" with raised beds (24\u201330 inches high) and adjacent turning space.\n**3.2 Volunteer Accessibility:**\n   - **Clause 3.2.1:** No resident shall be required to perform physical labor as a condition of plot tenure.\n   - **Clause 3.2.2:** Alternative volunteer roles must be offered for those unable to perform physical work. Examples include:\n     - *Administrative:* Managing the waitlist, updating the website, or handling correspondence.\n     - *Educational:* Leading workshops on composting, seed saving, or pest management.\n     - *Social:* Organizing community events, managing the tool library, or mentoring new gardeners.\n     - *Remote:* Designing flyers, managing social media, or translating materials.\n\n### Article IV: Community Standards and Conduct\n**4.1 Shade Management:**\n   - Tall plants (corn, sunflowers, trellised beans) must be planted on the north side of plots to minimize shading neighbors.\n   - Disputes over shade are resolved via the Conflict Resolution Process (Section 5).\n**4.2 Chemical Use:**\n   - **Prohibited:** Synthetic pesticides, herbicides, and fungicides.\n   - **Permitted:** Organic-certified products and integrated pest management (IPM) techniques.\n   - **Notification:** Residents must post a 48-hour notice before applying any spray, even organic, to allow neighbors to protect their produce.\n**4.3 Produce and Unattended Items:**\n   - Produce left unattended for >48 hours may be harvested by the Garden Committee and donated to a local food bank.\n   - Personal tools must be stored in the designated tool shed or removed from the garden after use.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Balanced Budget Proposal\n\n### Current Financial Status (Annual)\n| Item | Current Cost/Revenue | Notes |\n|------|----------------------|-------|\n| **Revenue** | | |\n| Plot Fees (40 households \u00d7 $35) | $1,400 | Current rate |\n| **Expenses** | | |\n| Irrigation | $900 | Water bills |\n| Tool Insurance | $250 | Liability coverage |\n| Compost | $500 | Bulk purchase |\n| **Total Expenses** | **$1,650** | |\n| **Net Balance** | **-$250** | **Deficit** |\n\n### Proposed Budget (Year 1)\nTo address the deficit and fund accessibility improvements, the following adjustments are proposed:\n\n| Item | Proposed Amount | Justification |\n|------|-----------------|---------------|\n| **Revenue** | | |\n| Plot Fees (40 households \u00d7 $45) | $1,800 | Increase of $10/year to cover deficit and inflation |\n| Grant Funding (Accessibility) | $500 | Apply for local community grants for raised beds |\n| **Total Revenue** | **$2,300**",
